					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tl333s" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1304786); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1304786&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200699" alt="TL333s" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1304786)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tl333s" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1304786); return false;" >TL333s</a></span> <span class="odate time_1530627997 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Jul 2018 14:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3860584">
									

<p>Not a good contest entry, but definitely a good article. I have a few thoughts, however.</p>
<p>First, the formatting is confusing, in two ways. The 'folder tabs' don't change appreciably when clicked, so it's hard to know which one you're on. (The tab itself moves up slightly, but as the text does not, I expect this is a coding mistake rather than an intended feature.) Second, with the second part of the email or whatever at the bottom, I assumed it was a continuation of the article and read it after reading the first folder tab, which made no sense at the time. I'm not sure how better you could position it.</p>
<p>I also feel like the footnotes in part four are 'narmy', I guess is the word. I don't even know what they're supposed to be. The depiction of the Foundation succumbing to this meme is pretty great &#8212; if nothing else, I really like the object itself and the memetic effect &#8212; but it maybe goes a little overboard. Mostly, like I said, I don't know what those footnotes are meant to represent, because usually footnotes are just that, not snatches of things overheard as an article is written.</p>
<p>Upvote after contest.</p>

